# Rank 1070 / CVE-2022-23639: crossbeam-utils' AtomicCell did its arithmetic by
# casting the cell's own storage to an atomic of the matching width and calling
# fetch_add on it, whether or not the type could be held in that atomic
# (CWE-362). impl_arithmetic held
#
#   let a = unsafe { &*(self.value.get() as *const $atomic) };
#   a.fetch_add(val, Ordering::AcqRel)
#
# and on a target where the type does not transmute to that atomic -- a 64-bit
# value on a platform whose 64-bit atomic is wider or differently aligned -- the
# reference is not to a valid atomic, so concurrent AtomicCell users race and the
# read-modify-write is not atomic at all. Pool commit 924436cc71 is the real fix,
# the merge of crossbeam#781, and its own diff is the fix: every arithmetic
# operation now asks can_transmute::<$t, $atomic>() first and falls back to
# taking the sharded write lock and doing the update by hand. The metadata check
# reported COMMIT_NONE because the advisory names a released version rather than
# a commit. No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, and this one is not a plumbing gap. The vulnerable and the fixed
# code perform the same cast on the same expression; what separates them is
# whether the target's layout lets the value live in that atomic, which is a
# question about type layout on a platform rather than about any expression in
# the file. No source, sink or control concept can carry it, and no presence
# pattern over the function can state it, because both revisions contain the cast
# and the fixed one merely reaches it under a condition. Neither is it a case
# where a language-level fact would settle it, as at rank 1066: the fact needed
# here is per-target and the code is inside a macro_rules body expanded once per
# integer width, so even the two sides of the condition are not distinct
# functions.
#
# What the scanner does report on this file is unrelated and worth stating so the
# row is not mistaken for coverage: three VYQL-MEM-006 at low confidence on the
# raw dereferences at atomic_cell.rs:598, 619 and 640, whose review condition is
# that a pointer-like value is dereferenced with no null exclusion. That fires on
# both revisions and describes neither the race nor the layout assumption; the
# only label in the file otherwise is code.IndexAccessSink on the lock table.
#
# The two snippets differ only in whether the transmute is checked, and both
# assert that unrelated row, which is the point: it is what the scanner has to
# say about this file either way.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-analysis-gap

test "rank1070: the cell's storage is cast to an atomic unconditionally"
  lang rust
  file crossbeam-utils/src/atomic/atomic_cell.rs
  expect VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
  use std::cell::UnsafeCell;

  pub struct AtomicCell<T> {
      value: UnsafeCell<T>,
  }

  impl AtomicCell<u64> {
      pub fn fetch_add(&self, val: u64) -> u64 {
          let a = unsafe { &*(self.value.get() as *const AtomicU64) };
          a.fetch_add(val, Ordering::AcqRel)
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1070: the same cast taken only when the type transmutes"
  lang rust
  file crossbeam-utils/src/atomic/atomic_cell.rs
  expect VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
  use std::cell::UnsafeCell;

  pub struct AtomicCell<T> {
      value: UnsafeCell<T>,
  }

  impl AtomicCell<u64> {
      pub fn fetch_add(&self, val: u64) -> u64 {
          if can_transmute::<u64, AtomicU64>() {
              let a = unsafe { &*(self.value.get() as *const AtomicU64) };
              a.fetch_add(val, Ordering::AcqRel)
          } else {
              let _guard = lock(self.value.get() as usize).write();
              let value = unsafe { &mut *(self.value.get()) };
              let old = *value;
              *value = value.wrapping_add(val);
              old
          }
      }
  }
  ```
